    <section class="hu-read-section" id="checks">
      <div class="hu-read-kicker">The process</div>
      <h2 class="hu-read-h2">What the checks missed</h2>
      <p class="hu-read-p">I'll grant the checks are good. Before every push, every page loads at nine screen sizes in both
      themes and the tests read every page for patient details. Before a game ships, a script plays its daily wall by
      thumb at four phone sizes. Most nights it all comes back clean and I believe it.</p>
      <p class="hu-read-p">Twice this week it was clean and wrong. The phone game was the first. The second turned up on
      October 16, when a review opened every tab of every Learn page. Nine of the ten charts on Laws and
      Paradoxes drew their labels at 7 pixels on a phone, and the phone check had passed the page, because it only ever
      read the tab that was open when the page loaded. Tonight it learned to open every tab. On its first run it found the
      flowchart in Request Routing drawing 74 labels at 9 pixels on a phone, so we held the launch a few hours and fixed it.</p>
      <p class="hu-read-p">So what else isn't it looking at? I don't know yet. Each of these checks exists because
      something got past the last one, and I expect the list to keep growing after launch.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hu-read-section" id="huddle">
      <div class="hu-read-kicker">The new game</div>
      <h2 class="hu-read-h2">Daily Huddle</h2>
      <p class="hu-read-p">Daily Huddle is a board of 19 hex tiles you cross from top to bottom by answering healthcare
      trivia. My first note on the demo was "this is all very hard, I have to google most of these." So it got easier.
      There are three lifelines now, each free once a board, and every number question became four answers to pick from.
      On October 16 the three minute clock went away too: your time to cross is the score, and since October 18 you can
      keep playing the rest of the board after you cross.</p>
      <p class="hu-read-p">The bank started at 1,057 questions and is 2,771 tonight. A lot of the new ones are written from
      the site's own data, so when a map picks up a new measure or the career tool adds a job, the next build turns it into
      questions. The clinical questions have rules. Drugs show up only as facts, and a dose question is math with every
      number already in it. The normal ranges stayed drafts until I had checked each one myself.</p>
      <p class="hu-read-p">There's a board for every day through next October, and every one of them can be crossed; we
      dealt all 365 to check. The dealer doesn't remember yesterday, though. Someone who plays every day will meet a
      question they've seen before within the first week, and by December about half of each board will be familiar.</p>
      <p class="hu-read-p">Will anybody still be playing on day 40? I can't tell from here, and I'm not sure two dailies on
      one shelf is the right number.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hu-read-section" id="headwall">
      <div class="hu-read-kicker">The renamed game</div>
      <h2 class="hu-read-h2">Headwall</h2>
      <p class="hu-read-p">Device Assembly is Headwall now, named for the panel of outlets over a hospital bed where the
      whole game happens. Old links and saved walls carry over. It also learned a rule from the bedside on October 15: any
      patient with an artificial airway gets a resuscitation bag on the headwall. The game didn't know that until I said
      it, and now a wall with an ET tube or a trach won't pass without one.</p>
      <p class="hu-read-p">It got quieter, too. My note was that it "should not jump to the hint section or say what the
      last move is during the play," and the whole point is that it's a puzzle. Outside the five training walls, a move
      that works says nothing now. The daily wall starts a year-long plan on October 25, the first day of RC Week, with a new
      room each Monday and a three-wall story each Sunday.</p>
    </section>

